{
  "narrative_body": "Iran announced it would reopen the Strait of Hormuz midway through a virtual summit of 49 nations called to demand exactly that — and Anthony Albanese, the prime minister, noted the timing was \"not accidental.\"\n\nThe summit, co-hosted by France, Britain, Germany, and Italy, produced consensus on de-escalation, free passage, and a ban on tolls or privatisation of the Strait. A follow-on conference in London was confirmed. For Australia, the meeting marked a shift from bilateral fuel scrambling to coalition leadership alongside European powers — a qualitatively different diplomatic posture than the one-on-one energy deals struck with Brunei, Singapore, and Malaysia in the days before.\n\nThose bilateral deals produced real outputs. Mr Albanese met Malaysian prime minister Anwar Ibrahim in Putrajaya and signed a Joint Statement on Energy Security along with an agricultural market access agreement — binding commitments, not statements of intent. Indonesia agreed to supply 250,000 tonnes of urea, covering roughly a fifth of Australia's remaining fertiliser needs for the current planting season. Brunei and South Korea together will deliver 100 million litres of diesel, underwritten by Export Finance Australia, with BP joining the underwriting arrangement. Australia's bargaining chip was food: the Sydney Morning Herald reported the government was trading agricultural export access for fuel — food for fuel.\n\nThe American president complicated matters. He told reporters he was \"not happy\" with Australia's contribution to the Hormuz effort, ignoring the defence spending increase announced days earlier. Mr Albanese pushed back at a press conference, stating there had been \"no new requests\" from Washington — directly contradicting the implication. The friction was public and explicit; managed within the alliance framework rather than outside it, but more assertive than anything Australia has said since the crisis began. The government also confirmed at the summit that it had deployed a Wedgetail E-7 aircraft to the UAE for air-defence operations, anchoring Australia's security contribution to the diplomatic effort.\n\nEven as it managed Washington's displeasure, the government locked in its largest peacetime defence spending commitment in history. Richard Marles, the defence minister, unveiled the 2026 National Defence Strategy at the National Press Club, pledging $53 billion additional over the decade toward a target of 3% of GDP by 2033. The headline figure requires a caveat: the strategy uses the NATO definition of defence spending, under which Australia is already at roughly 2.8%, making the real increase smaller than it sounds. The strategy's priorities — $12-15 billion on drones and counter-drone systems, new medium-range air defence, and AUKUS — reflect the current threat environment. But the same document revealed that the AUKUS submarine budget has blown out by a third, rising from $53-63 billion over the decade to $71-96 billion, driven by additional construction years and infrastructure at Osborne and Henderson. Mr Marles attributed the increase to a wider accounting scope. Michael Shoebridge of Strategic Analysis Australia was less sanguine, warning that the incoming Chief of the Defence Force, Mark Hammond, would be inheriting \"plans made before the Ukraine war, before the Iran war, and before Trump's aggressive unilateralism.\"\n\nThe Japan relationship produced the week's most concrete hardware milestone. Mr Marles and his Japanese counterpart Shinjiro Koizumi signed contracts for the first three of 11 Mogami-class frigates aboard the Maritime Self-Defence Force vessel JS Kumano during its visit to Melbourne. The A$10 billion deal is Japan's largest-ever defence export, the first of real scale since Tokyo ended its military export ban in 2014. The first three frigates will be built by Mitsubishi Heavy Industries in Japan, with delivery from 2029, before production shifts to the Henderson shipyard in Western Australia. Designed for anti-submarine warfare, surface strike, and air defence, they will patrol Australia's northern approaches and key maritime trade routes.\n\nThe refinery fire made the economic picture bleaker. A fire at the Viva Energy refinery in Geelong — one of only two in the country — broke out on the night of April 15 and was brought under control by the following noon. It cut petrol output by roughly 40%; diesel and aviation fuel continued at reduced levels. The government confirmed production was running at 80% diesel, 80% aviation fuel, and 60% petrol. Viva Energy said it would cover the shortfall through imports. The fire did not cause the crisis, but it compounded an already thin supply margin at the worst possible moment.\n\nThe Reserve Bank had already been warning that the terrain was deteriorating. Andrew Hauser, the deputy governor, speaking in New York, said he lacked \"high confidence\" that rates were at the right level and that stagflation risks had \"emerged\" — calling it \"a central banker's nightmare.\" Australia entered the oil shock with two rate rises already this year, taking the cash rate to 4.1%, and headline inflation is expected to hit roughly 5% in the second quarter. Markets price a 65% chance of a further rise in May, with rates peaking around 4.6% by year-end. Unemployment held steady at 4.3% in March, reinforcing the hawkish case. Consumer sentiment collapsed.\n\nThe week's defining domestic political moment was Angus Taylor, the opposition leader, who used a speech at the Menzies Research Centre — with John Howard in the audience — to announce a values-based immigration policy: mandatory social media screening for \"subversive intent,\" temporary protection visas, scaled-up deportations, and specific scrutiny of roughly 1,700 Gazan arrivals Mr Taylor described as presenting \"a clear risk to our country.\" He said migrants from liberal democracies were more likely to hold Australian values than those from \"places ruled by fundamentalists, extremists and dictators.\" Paul Keating, the former prime minister, called the policy \"cowardly and racist.\" One Nation's Pauline Hanson welcomed it as an endorsement of her platform. Inside the Liberal party, the Saturday Paper sourced MPs describing it as \"rank populism for a byelection\" and warning it would cost the Coalition votes in Bennelong, Parramatta, Reid, and Bradfield — all seats with large Asian-Australian communities. The policy was designed to eat into One Nation's support base; its immediate effect was to deepen the Coalition's internal fractures and give Ms Hanson a victory lap.\n\nQuieter but historically significant: Australia charged Ben Roberts-Smith, a Victoria Cross recipient, with five counts of war crime murder relating to the alleged killing of unarmed Afghan civilians between 2009 and 2012. It is the first time Australia has prosecuted one of its own soldiers for war crimes. Mr Roberts-Smith denied all charges and vowed to clear his name. The presiding judge noted the case was exceptional and that the accused might face \"possibly years and years\" before trial. The case builds on a 2023 Federal Court defamation ruling that found \"substantial truth\" in some murder allegations and a failed appeal in 2025. Of the 39 unlawful killings documented in the Brereton Report, this case touches on two — the gap between finding and prosecution remains wide.",
  "other_stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Australia's fuel crisis deepens: Albanese conducts SE Asia energy diplomacy, Geelong refinery fire, Hormuz summit, and Trump criticism",
      "summary": "The Iran war-driven global oil shock dominated Australian politics this week. PM Albanese travelled to Brunei and Malaysia to secure fuel and fertiliser supplies, the Geelong Viva Energy refinery caught fire reducing petrol production by 40%, Albanese joined a ~50-nation virtual summit co-hosted by Macron and Starmer on reopening the Strait of Hormuz, and Trump publicly criticised Australia for not doing enough to assist. Albanese rejected Trump's claim that new requests had been made and denied the country would move to fuel rationing. The Strait briefly reopened before Iran closed it again."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Angus Taylor's 'values-based' immigration policy draws racist charge from Keating and internal Liberal concern",
      "summary": "Opposition Leader Angus Taylor announced a hardline immigration policy pledging Trump-style social media vetting, deportation of migrants who fail 'Australian values' standards, and singling out Gazan arrivals as security risks. The policy drew a blistering attack from former PM Paul Keating labelling it 'cowardly and racist,' criticism from Liberal moderates who worry it will cost urban seats, and broad media debate. Taylor defended the policy as a values-first reset and unveiled it alongside former PM John Howard."
    },
    {
      "headline": "RBA signals further rate hikes as fuel shock risks stagflation; Deputy Governor Hauser warns of 'central banker's nightmare'",
      "summary": "RBA Deputy Governor Andrew Hauser gave multiple speeches warning that Australia entered the Iran oil shock 'already running hot,' that the central bank lacks 'high confidence' rates are at the right level, and that stagflation risks have emerged. Employment data for March came in broadly in line with forecasts (unemployment steady at 4.3%), reinforcing hawkish RBA policy expectations. Markets priced a ~65% chance of a further rate hike to 4.35% at the May meeting. Consumer sentiment collapsed."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Australia releases 2026 National Defence Strategy with $305–$425 billion decade-long spending commitment",
      "summary": "The Albanese government released its 2026 National Defence Strategy and Integrated Investment Program, pledging to boost defence spending to approximately 3% of GDP by 2033. Defence Minister Marles framed it as addressing Australia's 'most complex and threatening strategic circumstances since World War II.' The plan drew criticism from private sector investors who were unenthusiastic about proposals for superannuation funds to supplement defence spending. A separate analysis flagged tension between funding AUKUS and conventional force modernisation simultaneously."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Federal polls show Labor ahead of Coalition; One Nation slides from historic highs",
      "summary": "Multiple polling surveys released this week (Newspoll, Resolve Political Monitor, DemosAU, Morgan) showed Labor maintaining a lead on a two-party preferred basis, One Nation's primary vote dipping from historic peaks, and Angus Taylor's net approval declining. The polls were conducted during the week Albanese was abroad managing the energy crisis and Taylor launched his immigration policy. Labor's preferred PM lead remained at 46% vs Taylor's 37%."
    }
  ]
}

